<%# locals: (geometry_url:, name:, blob_url:, thumbnail:, download_url:, save:, port_writable:, create_url:, begin_url:, finalize_url:, parent_node_id:, source_node_id:, link_url:) %>
<%#
  The interactive Measure view — .lbrn/.lbrn2, .mage, .dxf, .svg. See measure_controller.js.

  Mirrors the 3D viewer's lazy pattern: nothing is fetched until the user clicks Measure, which
  matters because the payload reaches 281 KB gzipped on the densest real files and most pages are
  never measured.

  ONE PARTIAL FOR ALL FOUR FORMATS, with only the POSTER differing (below). The controller is
  format-agnostic — it reads a wire contract, not a file type — so a per-format partial would be
  four copies of one surface differing in a single img tag, which is the shape §14d's five
  `port_writable?` copies warn about.

  ONE PARTIAL FOR BOTH PAGES SINCE STAGE 4, S4-5, and every URL arrives as a strict local for that
  reason: a visitor's file page renders this same file with `geometry_url` pointing at the public
  reporter route, and it must not be able to reach a private route helper or `current_user` by
  accident (test/architecture_invariants_test.rb scans this directory). Read-only in the only sense
  that matters — Measure has never had a write control over the file it is measuring; the four save
  URLs below write an EXPORT, and a page with no session has nowhere to put one.

  WHICH IS WHAT `save` IS FOR, AND WHY IT DROPS THE CONTROLS RATHER THAN DISABLING THEM. The two
  Save twins (stage 4, S4-3) write that export into the MAKER's library through the two upload
  doors; a visitor has no library, so `save: false` renders neither the buttons nor the four write
  URLs — a disabled pair would be two dead controls, and rendering the URLs alone would put a
  private write path in a stranger's DOM for nothing. Print and Download SVG stay on both pages:
  both are client-side work over a payload the visitor has already fetched, and withholding them
  would cost story 5.5 the one thing it asks for. `measure_controller.js#refreshPrintBar` asks
  `hasSaveSvgButtonTarget` for exactly this branch — the guard is real here, unlike the ones its
  own comment refuses for the always-rendered controls beside them.
%>
<div class="measure-view"
     data-controller="measure"
     data-measure-url-value="<%= geometry_url %>"
     data-measure-name-value="<%= name %>"
     <%#
       SAVE TO LIBRARY (stage 4, S4-3; story 2.12). Everything Measure produced was a browser
       download and nothing landed on the share, so an exported outline lived in ~/Downloads while
       the file it came from lived on the NAS.

       THE TWO DOORS, PICKED BY THE DEPLOYMENT AND NOT BY THE USER (§14a). `port_writable` names
       WHICH DOOR the bytes go in: true (share, s3) means one multipart POST to FilesController
       #create; false (chunk_remote) means the two-phase presigned flow, where the bytes go browser
       -> storage and never through Rails (§15). Both are rendered as values rather than one
       branch, because the controller has to choose in JavaScript anyway and
       importer_controller.js already picks between the same two functions the same way.

       THE DESTINATION IS THE SOURCE FILE'S OWN PARENT, so an export lands beside the thing it was
       exported from. Empty at the library root, which lib/chunk_upload reads as "no parent".

       ALL SEVEN ARE INSIDE THE `save` BRANCH (stage 4, S4-5): the two buttons below are what reads
       them, and a page that renders neither must not carry an upload door either. The two lineage
       values joined them in S4-8 for the same reason — a visitor writes nothing, so a link door in
       a stranger's DOM would be a private write path rendered for nobody.
     %>
     <% if save %>
     data-measure-port-writable-value="<%= port_writable %>"
     data-measure-create-url-value="<%= create_url %>"
     data-measure-begin-url-value="<%= begin_url %>"
     data-measure-finalize-url-value="<%= finalize_url %>"
     data-measure-parent-node-id-value="<%= parent_node_id %>"
     <%#
       LINEAGE FOR A SAVE (stage 4, S4-8; story 3.2). Everything Measure saves is DERIVED from this
       file, so the save records that as it lands — the maker who opens the export a month later is
       one click from the .lbrn2 it came out of, and the panel's `×` on that page is what withdraws
       the link again (plan decision 2).

       `__ID__` IS A TEMPLATE THE SERVER WRITES, and the id is substituted in the browser: the new
       node does not exist until the upload answers, and this partial must not learn a route —
       every URL it needs arrives as a strict local, which is what keeps a private path off a
       visitor's page (architecture_invariants_test.rb scans this directory for `*_path`/`*_url`).
     %>
     data-measure-source-node-id-value="<%= source_node_id %>"
     data-measure-link-url-value="<%= link_url %>"
     <% end %>>
  <div class="measure-stage" data-measure-target="stage"></div>
  <div class="measure-bar">
    <span class="measure-dims" data-measure-target="dims"></span>
    <span class="measure-launch">
      <button type="button" class="btn small" data-action="measure#load">Measure</button>
    </span>
    <span class="measure-actions">
      <%#
        THERE IS NO "Select parts" TOGGLE HERE ANY MORE (2026-09-03, at the user's request).
        Selecting is what a click DOES — every click on a contour marks it, a second click on the
        same contour drops it, and a click on empty space clears the lot — so the button that used
        to arm that behaviour, its `aria-pressed`, and the `selectToggle` target are gone rather
        than hidden. See measure_controller.js's `pick` and §14h.
      %>
      <%#
        THE PAPER OPTIONS ARE POPULATED BY THE CONTROLLER FROM print_sheet.js's PAPERS table, not
        listed here. A second copy of the paper list in ERB is a second definition that drifts, and
        this select only ever appears once the controller has run anyway.
      %>
      <select class="measure-paper" aria-label="Paper size"
              data-measure-target="paper" data-action="measure#paperChanged"></select>
      <button type="button" class="btn small" data-action="measure#print"
              data-measure-target="printButton">Print all</button>
      <%#
        SAVE, BESIDE PRINT. Constant text, for the reason the SVG button's is constant: the bar
        carries seven controls now and a second count-bearing label beside "Print 3 parts" is
        noise. The accessible name tracks the target, written by the same refreshPrintBar() from
        the same targetNoun(), so the four labels can only drift together.
      %>
      <% if save %>
        <button type="button" class="btn small ghost" data-action="measure#savePrint"
                data-measure-target="savePrintButton"
                aria-label="Save all as a print sheet in the library"
                title="Save all as a print sheet in the library">Save sheet</button>
      <% end %>
      <%#
        VISIBLE TEXT IS CONSTANT, THE ACCESSIBLE NAME IS NOT. The bar already carries SIX other
        controls since stage 4, S4-3 added the two Save twins (it was four), so a second
        count-bearing label beside "Print 3 parts" is noise — but the button
        must still say what it will do, so refreshPrintBar() writes aria-label/title from the same
        targetNoun() the Print label uses. The literals here are the at-rest state the controller
        writes on draw(); they must agree with it, exactly as "Print all" does above.

        THE TEXT READS "Download SVG" AS OF 2026-09-03, at the user's request; it was "SVG". Still
        constant, and still not the count — a one-word button beside a paper picker did not say
        what it would produce.
      %>
      <button type="button" class="btn small ghost" data-action="measure#downloadSvg"
              data-measure-target="svgButton"
              aria-label="Download all as SVG" title="Download all as SVG">Download SVG</button>
      <% if save %>
        <button type="button" class="btn small ghost" data-action="measure#saveSvg"
                data-measure-target="saveSvgButton"
                aria-label="Save all as SVG in the library"
                title="Save all as SVG in the library">Save SVG</button>
      <% end %>
      <button type="button" class="btn small ghost" data-action="measure#reset">Reset</button>
      <button type="button" class="btn small ghost" aria-label="Return to image"
              data-action="measure#showPoster">✕ Image</button>
    </span>
  </div>
  <div class="measure-fallback" data-measure-target="fallback">
    <%#
      THE POSTER: what the page shows at rest, before Measure is clicked, and what ✕ Image
      returns to.

      AN SVG IS ITS OWN POSTER — served from the byte route into an img tag, exactly as the
      :image branch does. That is NOT a relaxation of the rule that this app never inlines user
      SVG (§15): an img src is a replaced element, so scripts in the document do not run and it
      cannot reach this page's DOM. Inlining the markup is what is forbidden, and nothing here
      does that — the Measure drawing itself is built from parsed coordinates, never from the
      file's own markup.

      Every other format posts its server-rendered thumbnail, reproducing ALL THREE states the
      :thumbnail branch handles, because these extensions no longer reach it.
    %>
    <% if File.extname(name).downcase == ".svg" %>
      <img src="<%= blob_url %>" alt="<%= name %>" class="preview-image">
    <% elsif thumbnail&.status == "ready" && thumbnail.asset.attached? %>
      <img src="<%= url_for(thumbnail.asset) %>" alt="Preview of <%= name %>" class="preview-image">
    <% elsif %w[pending processing].include?(thumbnail&.status) %>
      <p class="muted">Generating preview…</p>
    <% else %>
      <p class="muted">No inline preview for this file.</p>
      <%= link_to "Download #{name}", download_url, class: "btn" %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>
